<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jack Hall
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics, KTH, 100 44 Stockholm, Sweden
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:jackhall@kth.se">jackhall@kth.se</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: March 8, 2013</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that cohomology and base change holds for algebraic stacks, generalizing work of Brochard in the tame case. We also show that Hom-spaces on algebraic stacks are represented by abelian cones, generalizing results of Grothendieck, Brochard, Olsson, Lieblich, and Roth–Starr. To accomplish all of this, we prove that a wide class of Ext-functors in algebraic geometry are coherent (in the sense of M. Auslander).</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2010 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>Primary 14F05; Secondary 13D09, 14D20, 14D23
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first main result is the following version of
cohomology and base change.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmainthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmainthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A</span></span><span id="Thmmainthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmainthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmainthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a morphism of locally noetherian algebraic stacks <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>
which is separated and locally of finite type, <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>Coh</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{Coh}({X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{Coh}({X})</annotation></semantics></math> which is properly supported
and flat over <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. For each integer <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and morphism of
noetherian algebraic stacks <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:T\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:T\to S</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a
natural base change morphism:</span></p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{q}(T):g^{*}\mathcal{E}xt^{{q}}({f};{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }},{\mathcal{{N}}})\to\mathcal{E}xt^{{q}}({f_{T}};{\mathsf{L}{(g_{X})}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }},{g_{X}^{*}\mathcal{{N}}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{q}(T):g^{*}\mathcal{E}xt^{{q}}({f};{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }},{\mathcal{{N}}})\to\mathcal{E}xt^{{q}}({f_{T}};{\mathsf{L}{(g_{X})}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }},{g_{X}^{*}\mathcal{{N}}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmmainthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}:X_{T}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}:X_{T}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pullback of <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{X}:X_{T}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{X}:X_{T}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pullback of <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Now fix <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in|S|</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmmainthm1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{q}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{q}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective.</span></p>
<ol id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, there exists an open neighbourhood </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
of </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in|S|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that for any </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:T\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:T\to S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> factoring through
</span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the map </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{q}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{q}(T)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{q+1}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{q+1}(s)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is surjective,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the coherent </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-module
</span><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}xt^{{q+1}}({f};{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }},{\mathcal{{N}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}xt^{{q+1}}({f};{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }},{\mathcal{{N}}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is free at </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm1" title="Theorem A. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for projective morphisms
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA</a>, III.7.7.5]</cite>, the
essential point was to corepresent the
relevant functors by bounded above complexes of coherent sheaves on
<math id="Sx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the following notion was indispensible: for a
noetherian scheme <math id="Sx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, a functor <math id="Sx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{QCoh}({S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{QCoh}({S})</annotation></semantics></math> is
<em id="Sx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">corepresentable by a complex</em> if it is of the form <math id="Sx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{J}}\mapsto\mathcal{H}^{{0}}(\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathcal{{{Q}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{J}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{J}}\mapsto\mathcal{H}^{{0}}(\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathcal{{{Q}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{J}}))</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Sx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{Q}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>Coh</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{Q}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Brochard noticed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bro12</a>, App. A]</cite>, however,
that corepresenting the cohomology functors of a non-tame stack by
complexes is quite subtle—the problem is that these stacks tend to
have infinite cohomological dimension. In particular, the “Mumford Lemma”
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum70</a>, Lem. 5.1]</cite> is no longer applicable. Our next main
result shows that this problem can be circumvented when one has duality.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmainthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmainthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B</span></span><span id="Thmmainthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmainthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmainthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a noetherian algebraic stack <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> which admits a dualizing
complex <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{K}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{K}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }</annotation></semantics></math>, and a morphism of algebraic stacks <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> which is proper. Let <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>Coh</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{b}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>Coh</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{b}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }</annotation></semantics></math> has finite tor-dimension over <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>,
then there exists a quasi-isomorphism:</span></p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{R}{f}_{*}\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}(\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}^{\mathsf{L}}\mathsf{L}{f}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}\mathcal{{{I}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ })\simeq\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathcal{{{E}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }_{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }},\mathcal{{{I}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ })\quad\forall\,\mathcal{{{I}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{QCoh}}^{+}(S)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>𝖫</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>𝖱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup></mrow><mtext>    </mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>QCoh</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{R}{f}_{*}\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}(\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}^{\mathsf{L}}\mathsf{L}{f}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}\mathcal{{{I}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ })\simeq\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathcal{{{E}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }_{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }},\mathcal{{{I}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ })\quad\forall\,\mathcal{{{I}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{QCoh}}^{+}(S),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmmainthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">natural in <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{I}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{I}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</span></p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{E}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }_{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }}\simeq\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathsf{R}{f}_{*}\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}(\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}^{\mathsf{L}}\mathsf{L}{f}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}\mathcal{{{K}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }),\mathcal{{{K}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ })\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(S)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup></mrow><mtext>    </mtext></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>𝖱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>𝖫</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>Coh</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{E}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }_{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }}\simeq\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathsf{R}{f}_{*}\mathsf{R}\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}(\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}^{\mathsf{L}}\mathsf{L}{f}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}\mathcal{{{K}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }),\mathcal{{{K}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ })\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(S).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmmainthm2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In addition, if <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\simeq\mathcal{{N}}[0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\simeq\mathcal{{N}}[0]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{Coh}({X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{Coh}({X})</annotation></semantics></math> is flat over <math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then the formation of
<math id="Thmmainthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{E}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }_{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup></mrow><mtext>    </mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{E}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }_{\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with base change.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since any separated scheme of finite type over a Gorenstein ring
(e.g. <math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\spec\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\spec\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> or a field) and the spectrum of any maximal-adically
complete noetherian local ring admits a dualizing complex. Thus, Theorem
<a href="#Thmmainthm2" title="Theorem B. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> covers most cases encountered in practice
(in particular, it generalizes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">BF97</a>, Lem. 6.1]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We wish to point out, however, that the collection of functors
<math id="Sx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{QCoh}({S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{QCoh}({S})</annotation></semantics></math> which are
corepresentable by a complex is poorly behaved. Indeed, it is
not even closed under direct summands <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har98</a>, Prop. 4.6 &amp; Ex. 5.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The following generalization of functors which are corepresentable by
a complex was considered by M. Auslander <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aus66</a>]</cite> in order to
correct such deficiencies. For an
affine (not necessarily noetherian) scheme <math id="Sx1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, a functor <math id="Sx1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{Ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐀𝐛</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{Ab}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Sx1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coherent</em> if there exists a morphism of
quasicoherent <math id="Sx1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="Sx1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{K}}_{1}\to\mathcal{{K}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{K}}_{1}\to\mathcal{{K}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that
for all <math id="Sx1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{I}}\in\mathbf{QCoh}({S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{I}}\in\mathbf{QCoh}({S})</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a natural isomorphism of
abelian groups:</p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathcal{{I}})\cong\coker(\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathcal{{K}}_{2},\mathcal{{I}})\to\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathcal{{K}}_{1},\mathcal{{I}}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathcal{{I}})\cong\coker(\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathcal{{K}}_{2},\mathcal{{I}})\to\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{S}}(\mathcal{{K}}_{1},\mathcal{{I}})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Note that if <math id="Sx1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is noetherian, the coherent functors of R. Hartshorne
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har98</a>]</cite> are precisely those coherent functors of M. Auslander
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aus66</a>]</cite> which preserve direct limits.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In any case, the
collection of coherent functors is very well-behaved: it is an
abelian category which is closed under extensions and inverse
limits—precisely the sort of properties that are convenient
to have at one’s disposal when performing induction arguments. Thus, using
Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm2" title="Theorem B. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, we can prove the following Theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmainthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmainthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem C</span></span><span id="Thmmainthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmainthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmainthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix an affine scheme <math id="Thmmainthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, a morphism of algebraic stacks <math id="Thmmainthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> which is separated and locally of finite presentation,
<math id="Thmmainthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{QCoh}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>QCoh</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{QCoh}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmmainthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{QCoh}({X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{QCoh}({X})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="Thmmainthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> of finite
presentation, properly supported and flat over <math id="Thmmainthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the functor:</span></p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}(\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{N}}\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}^{\mathsf{L}}\mathsf{L}{f}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}(-)):\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{Ab}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>𝖫</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐀𝐛</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}(\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{N}}\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}^{\mathsf{L}}\mathsf{L}{f}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}(-)):\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{Ab}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmmainthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is coherent.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We wish to emphasize that Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>
eliminates from Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm2" title="Theorem B. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> the finiteness
hypotheses on <math id="Sx1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="Sx1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is permitted to be non-noetherian) and on
<math id="Sx1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="Sx1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }</annotation></semantics></math> can be unbounded with quasicoherent
cohomology). Moreover, the employment of Auslander’s definition of
coherent functors is essential for the proof and truth of Theorem
<a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. If <math id="Sx1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is noetherian and <math id="Sx1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>Coh</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{-}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, then Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>
implies that the functor is coherent in the sense of Hartshorne (see Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 1.1. ‣ 1. Derived categories of sheaves on algebraic stacks ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm1" title="Theorem A. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> is proved by combining a clever
result of A. Ogus and G. Bergman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">OB72</a>, Cor. 5.1]</cite>,
Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, and some general vanishing results
for coherent functors. An interesting application of Theorem
<a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> is the following:
given a scheme <math id="Sx1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, an algebraic <math id="Sx1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-stack <math id="Sx1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{QCoh}({X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{QCoh}({X})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <math id="Sx1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-presheaf
<math id="Sx1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Hom}_{\mathcal{O}_{X}/S}(\mathcal{{M}},\mathcal{{N}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Hom</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Hom}_{\mathcal{O}_{X}/S}(\mathcal{{M}},\mathcal{{N}})</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="Sx1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Sx1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\Hom}_{\mathcal{O}_{X}/S}(\mathcal{{M}},\mathcal{{N}})[T\xrightarrow{\tau}S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Hom</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜏</mo></mover><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\Hom}_{\mathcal{O}_{X}/S}(\mathcal{{M}},\mathcal{{N}})[T\xrightarrow{\tau}S]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Sx1.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}}}(\tau_{X}^{*}\mathcal{{M}},\tau_{X}^{*}\mathcal{{N}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}}}(\tau_{X}^{*}\mathcal{{M}},\tau_{X}^{*}\mathcal{{N}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{X}:X\times_{S}T\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{X}:X\times_{S}T\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection. Then, we prove</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmainthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmainthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem D</span></span><span id="Thmmainthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmainthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmainthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a scheme <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and a morphism of algebraic stacks <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>, which
is separated and locally of finite presentation. Let <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{QCoh}({X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{N}}\in\mathbf{QCoh}({X})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> of finite presentation, flat
over <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, with support proper over <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,
<math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Hom}_{\mathcal{O}_{X}/S}(\mathcal{{M}},\mathcal{{N}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Hom</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Hom}_{\mathcal{O}_{X}/S}(\mathcal{{M}},\mathcal{{N}})</annotation></semantics></math> is
representable by an abelian cone over <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> (which is, in particular,
affine over <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>). If <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite presentation, then
<math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Hom}_{\mathcal{O}_{X}/S}(\mathcal{{M}},\mathcal{{N}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Hom</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Hom}_{\mathcal{O}_{X}/S}(\mathcal{{M}},\mathcal{{N}})</annotation></semantics></math> is finitely presented over <math id="Thmmainthm4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We wish to emphasize that Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm4" title="Theorem D. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> is
completely elementary once Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> is
known. Using different techniques to that employed in Theorem
<a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, we prove a coherence result when nothing
is assumed to be flat (at the expense of making the diagonal finite).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmainthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmainthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E</span></span><span id="Thmmainthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmainthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmainthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix an affine and noetherian scheme <math id="Thmmainthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, a morphism of algebraic
stacks <math id="Thmmainthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> which is locally of finite type with finite
diagonal, <math id="Thmmainthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{QCoh}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>QCoh</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{QCoh}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmmainthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{b}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>Coh</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\in\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Coh}}^{b}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmmainthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }</annotation></semantics></math> has properly supported cohomology sheaves
over <math id="Thmmainthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then the functor:</span></p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}(\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}^{\mathsf{L}}\mathsf{L}{f}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}(-)):\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{Ab}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>𝖫</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐀𝐛</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}(\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ },\mathcal{{{N}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}^{\mathsf{L}}\mathsf{L}{f}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{*}(-)):\mathbf{QCoh}({S})\to\mathbf{Ab}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmmainthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is coherent.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We wish to emphasize that Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm5" title="Theorem E. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> is
independent of Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm2" title="Theorem B. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Relation with other work</h3>

<div id="Sx1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hal12</a>]</cite>, coherent functors featured prominently
in a criteria for algebraicity of stacks. Thus Theorem
<a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> can be used to show that certain stacks are
algebraic [<em id="Sx1.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">op. cit.</em>, §§8–9]. Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm4" title="Theorem D. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> can
also be used to show that many algebraic stacks of interest have affine
diagonals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hal12</a>, §§8-9]</cite>—generalizing and
simplifying the existing work of M. Olsson
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ols06</a>, Prop. 5.10]</cite>, M. Lieblich <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lie06</a>, Prop. 2.3]</cite>,
and M. Roth and J. Starr <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">RS09</a>, Thm. 2.1]</cite>. In
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">HR12b</a>]</cite>, Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm4" title="Theorem D. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> is
combined with the absolute approximation results of D. Rydh
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ryd09</a>]</cite> to show that Hilbert stacks and Quot spaces exist
without finiteness assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Results of A. Grothendieck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA</a>, III.7.7.8-9]</cite>, A. Altman and
S. Kleiman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK80</a>, 1.1]</cite>, and S. Brochard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bro12</a>, Prop. A.4.3]</cite> demonstrate that Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm1" title="Theorem A. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> holds
when <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, tame, and flat, and <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is flat over <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as well as being the cokernel of a map vector bundles. For
example, if <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, or more generally, <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is tame and has
the resolution property, Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm1" title="Theorem A. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> is known to hold
for any coherent sheaf <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is flat over <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After completing this paper we also located in the literature two very nice
papers of H. Flenner addressing similar results for analytic
spaces. In particular, if <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is excellent and of finite Krull
dimension, then Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm1" title="Theorem A. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> follows from the results
of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fle81</a>, §7]</cite> and Theorems
<a href="#Thmmainthm2" title="Theorem B. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and <a href="#Thmmainthm4" title="Theorem D. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> are the main results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fle82</a>]</cite>
(this is in the analytic category, however, thus <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
assumed to be coherent in Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm4" title="Theorem D. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). Without
further assumptions on <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>    </mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{{{M}}}^{\,\begin{picture}(0.0,-2.0)\circle*{1.5}\end{picture}\ }</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are
flat over <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK80</a>, §1]</cite>) we cannot see how
Theorem <a href="#Thmmainthm1" title="Theorem A. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> can be easily reduced to the case where
<math id="Sx1.SSx1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> meets Flenner’s hypotheses. In fact, we use coherent functors to
accomplish this descent, which is effectively the content of Theorem
<a href="#Thmmainthm3" title="Theorem C. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. This has no counterpart in the analytic
category where everything is excellent, coherent, and admits a
dualizing complex. We do not believe that Theorem
<a href="#Thmmainthm5" title="Theorem E. ‣ Introduction ‣ Cohomology and base change for algebraic stacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> has been considered previously.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Assumptions, conventions, and notations</h3>

<div id="Sx1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a scheme <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T|</annotation></semantics></math> the underlying topological space
(with the Zariski topology) and <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> the (Zariski) sheaf of rings
on <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T|</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in|T|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in|T|</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(t)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the residue field.
Denote by <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{QCoh}({T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{QCoh}({T})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Coh}({T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Coh}({T})</annotation></semantics></math>) the abelian category of
quasicoherent (resp. coherent) sheaves on the scheme <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Sch}/{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐒𝐜𝐡</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Sch}/{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the category of schemes over <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. The big étale
site over <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> will be denoted by <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{Sch}/{T})_{\mathrm{\acute{E}t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒𝐜𝐡</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{Sch}/{T})_{\mathrm{\acute{E}t}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a ring <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and an <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, denote the quasicoherent
<math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\spec A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\spec A}</annotation></semantics></math>-module associated to <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote
the abelian category of all (resp. coherent) <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules by <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Mod}({A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Mod}({A})</annotation></semantics></math>
(resp. <math id="Sx1.SSx2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Coh}({A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂𝐨𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Coh}({A})</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will assume throughout that all schemes, algebraic spaces, and
algebraic stacks have quasicompact and quasiseparated diagonals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="Sx1.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I would like to thank R. Ile and D. Rydh for several interesting and
supportive discussions.</p>
</div>
